<p>Cuban President Miguel Díaz-Canel said Cuba recently held talks with US officials to find solutions to bilateral differences, marking Havana’s first public acknowledgment in more than a decade of formal contacts with Washington. He said the discussions were grounded in dialogue, equality, respect for each country’s political system, and Cuba’s sovereignty and self-determination. Separately, a White House official said President Trump has said the US is talking to Cuba and that a deal could be done easily. The talks come as Cuba struggles through a severe fuel and electricity crisis that Cuban officials tie to US restrictions on oil supplies. Multiple reports describe frequent blackouts, fuel shortages, and broader economic strain across the island. In a small sign of possible de-escalation, Cuba also said it would release 51 prisoners as a goodwill gesture, suggesting both sides may be testing whether dialogue can ease tensions even as major differences remain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9ECeh_BuJBH_JOVZzPFsn8dty4zXS8aBApHI_758i2kFjAGBmNOFdM2uJTI6C4Zct-DwccLoZfD-o8GglDVt_3e5e6wJHk-ITlCAbu0OcA_f1yXr8YelZsykMJp978aF-eN24SWx6TtiWboW8eceJiymSXb4IiA-gfBpUspc3F8lI_YoROU9cyA5JiooYgQ' alt='President Miguel Díaz-Canel of Cuba speaking in Havana in January.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Unclear format: Díaz-Canel confirmed contacts with Washington but did not say when or where they happened, or which officials took part.</li></ul><blockquote>These talks have been aimed at finding solutions through dialogue to the bilateral differences we have between the two nations - Miguel Díaz-Canel</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Miguel Díaz-Canel: He presented the contacts as a chance to move Havana and Washington away from confrontation after years of hostility. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1981928/us-cuba-open-talks-amid-trumps-threats'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>White House: A White House official said President Trump believes Cuba’s leaders should agree 